## sn4.21
sn4.21:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: Thus have I heard.
sn4.21:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Sakyans near Silāvatī. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling among the Sakyans at Silavati.
sn4.21:1.3 | S: Now at that time several mendicants were meditating not far from the Buddha, diligent, keen, and resolute. | T: Now on that occasion a number of bhikkhus were dwelling not far from the Blessed One—diligent, ardent, and resolute.
sn4.21:1.4 | S: Then Māra the Wicked manifested in the form of a brahmin with a large matted dreadlock, wearing an antelope hide. He was old, bent double, wheezing, and held a staff made of cluster fig tree wood. He went up to those mendicants and said, | T: Then Mara the Evil One manifested himself in the form of a brahmin, with a large matted topknot, clad in an antelope hide, old, crooked like a roof bracket, wheezing, holding a staff of udumbara wood. He approached those bhikkhus and said to them:
sn4.21:1.5 | S: “You’ve gone forth while young, reverends.  With pristine black hair, you’re blessed with youth, in the prime of life, and you’ve never flirted with sensual pleasures. | T: “You, sirs, have gone forth while young, lads with black hair, endowed with the blessing of youth, in the prime of life, without having dallied with sensual pleasures.
sn4.21:1.6 | S: Enjoy human sensual pleasures. | T: Enjoy human sensual pleasures, sirs;
sn4.21:1.7 | S: Don’t give up what is apparent in the present to chase after what takes effect over time.” | T: do not abandon what is directly visible in order to pursue what takes time.”
sn4.21:1.8 | S: “Brahmin, that’s not what we’re doing. | T: “We have not abandoned what is directly visible, brahmin, in order to pursue what takes time.
sn4.21:1.9 | S: We’re giving up what takes effect over time to chase after what is apparent in the present. | T: We have abandoned what takes time in order to pursue what is directly visible.
sn4.21:1.10 | S: For the Buddha says that sensual pleasures take effect over time; they give much suffering and distress, and they are all the more full of drawbacks. | T: For the Blessed One, brahmin, has stated that sensual pleasures are time-consuming, full of suffering, full of despair, and the danger in them is still greater,
sn4.21:1.11 | S: But this teaching is apparent in the present life, immediately effective, inviting inspection, relevant, so that sensible people can know it for themselves.” | T: while this Dhamma is directly visible, immediate, inviting one to come and see, applicable, to be personally experienced by the wise.”
sn4.21:1.12 | S: When they had spoken, Māra the Wicked shook his head, waggled his tongue, raised his eyebrows until his brow puckered in three furrows, and left leaning on his staff. | T: When this was said, Mara the Evil One shook his head, lolled his tongue, knit his brow into three furrows, and departed leaning on his staff.
sn4.21:2.1 | S: Then those mendicants went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and told him what had happened. The Buddha said, | T: Then those bhikkhus approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, sat down to one side, and reported everything in full. The Blessed One said:
