sn46.54:13.4 | S: If they wish: ‘May I meditate perceiving the repulsive in the unrepulsive,’ that’s what they do. … | T: If he wishes: ‘May I dwell perceiving the repulsive in the unrepulsive,’ he dwells perceiving the repulsive therein….
sn46.54:13.5 | S: If they wish: ‘May I meditate staying equanimous, mindful and aware, shunning both the repulsive and the unrepulsive,’ that’s what they do. | T: If he wishes: ‘Avoiding both the unrepulsive and the repulsive, may I dwell equanimously, mindful and clearly comprehending,’ then he dwells therein equanimously, mindful and clearly comprehending.
sn46.54:13.6 | S: Or else, going totally beyond perceptions of form, with the disappearance of perceptions of impingement, not focusing on perceptions of diversity, aware that ‘space is infinite’, they enter and remain in the dimension of infinite space. | T: Or else, with the complete transcendence of perceptions of forms, with the passing away of perceptions of sensory impingement, with nonattention to perceptions of diversity, aware that ‘space is infinite,’ he enters and dwells in the base of the infinity of space.
sn46.54:13.7 | S: The apex of the heart’s release by compassion is the dimension of infinite space, I say, for a wise mendicant here who has not penetrated to a higher freedom. | T: Bhikkhus, the liberation of mind by compassion has the base of the infinity of space as its culmination, I say, for a wise bhikkhu here who has not penetrated to a superior liberation.
sn46.54:14.1 | S: And how is the heart’s release by rejoicing developed? What is its destination, apex, fruit, and end? | T: “And how, bhikkhus, is the liberation of the mind by altruistic joy developed? What does it have as its destination, its culmination, its fruit, its final goal?
sn46.54:14.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant develops the heart’s release by rejoicing together with the awakening factors of mindfulness, investigation of principles, energy, rapture, tranquility, immersion, | T: Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu develops the enlightenment factor of mindfulness accompanied by altruistic joy … the enlightenment factor of equanimity accompanied by altruistic joy,
sn46.54:14.3 | S: and equanimity, which rely on seclusion, fading away, and cessation, and ripen as letting go. | T: based upon seclusion, dispassion, and cessation, maturing in release.
sn46.54:14.4 | S: If they wish: ‘May I meditate perceiving the repulsive in the unrepulsive,’ that’s what they do. … | T: If he wishes: ‘May I dwell perceiving the repulsive in the unrepulsive,’ he dwells perceiving the repulsive therein….
sn46.54:14.5 | S: If they wish: ‘May I meditate staying equanimous, mindful and aware, shunning both the repulsive and the unrepulsive,’ that’s what they do. | T: If he wishes: ‘Avoiding both the unrepulsive and the repulsive, may I dwell equanimously, mindful and clearly comprehending, ’ then he dwells therein equanimously, mindful and clearly comprehending.
sn46.54:14.6 | S: Or else, going totally beyond the dimension of infinite space, aware that ‘consciousness is infinite’, they enter and remain in the dimension of infinite consciousness. | T: Or else, by completely transcending the base of the infinity of space, aware that ‘consciousness is infinite,’ he enters and dwells in the base of the infinity of consciousness.
sn46.54:14.7 | S: The apex of the heart’s release by rejoicing is the dimension of infinite consciousness, I say, for a wise mendicant here who has not penetrated to a higher freedom. | T: Bhikkhus, the liberation of mind by altruistic joy has the base of the infinity of consciousness as its culmination, I say, for a wise bhikkhu here who has not penetrated to a superior liberation.
sn46.54:15.1 | S: And how is the heart’s release by equanimity developed? What is its destination, apex, fruit, and end? | T: “And how, bhikkhus, is the liberation of the mind by equanimity developed? What does it have as its destination, its culmination, its fruit, its final goal?
sn46.54:15.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant develops the heart’s release by equanimity together with the awakening factors of mindfulness, investigation of principles, energy, rapture, tranquility, immersion, | T: Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu develops the enlightenment factor of mindfulness accompanied by equanimity … the enlightenment factor of equanimity accompanied by equanimity,
sn46.54:15.3 | S: and equanimity, which rely on seclusion, fading away, and cessation, and ripen as letting go. | T: based upon seclusion, dispassion, and cessation, maturing in release.
sn46.54:15.4 | S: If they wish: ‘May I meditate perceiving the repulsive in the unrepulsive,’ that’s what they do. | T: If he wishes: ‘May I dwell perceiving the repulsive in the unrepulsive,’ he dwells perceiving the repulsive therein….
sn46.54:15.5 | S: If they wish: ‘May I meditate perceiving the unrepulsive in the repulsive,’ that’s what they do. | T: (none)
sn46.54:15.6 | S: If they wish: ‘May I meditate perceiving the repulsive in the unrepulsive and the repulsive,’ that’s what they do. | T: (none)
sn46.54:15.7 | S: If they wish: ‘May I meditate perceiving the unrepulsive in the repulsive and the unrepulsive,’ that’s what they do. | T: (none)
sn46.54:15.8 | S: If they wish: ‘May I meditate staying equanimous, mindful and aware, shunning both the repulsive and the unrepulsive,’ that’s what they do. | T: If he wishes: ‘Avoiding both the unrepulsive and the repulsive, may I dwell equanimously, mindful and clearly comprehending,’ then he dwells therein equanimously, mindful and clearly comprehending.
sn46.54:15.9 | S: Or else, going totally beyond the dimension of infinite consciousness, aware that ‘there is nothing at all’, they enter and remain in the dimension of nothingness. | T: Or else, by completely transcending the base of the infinity of consciousness, aware that ‘there is nothing,’ he enters and dwells in the base of nothingness.
sn46.54:15.10 | S: The apex of the heart’s release by equanimity is the dimension of nothingness, I say, for a wise mendicant here who has not penetrated to a higher freedom.” | T: Bhikkhus, the liberation of mind by equanimity has the base of nothingness as its culmination, I say, for a wise bhikkhu here who has not penetrated to a superior liberation.”
sn46.54:15.11 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.22
an4.22:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, this one time, when I was first awakened, I was staying in Uruvelā at the goatherd’s banyan tree on the bank of the Nerañjarā River. | T: “Bhikkhus, on one occasion I was dwelling at Uruvelā, by the goatherds’ banyan tree on the bank of the Neranjarā River, just after I had attained full enlightenment.
an4.22:1.2 | S: Then several old brahmins—elderly and senior, who were advanced in years and had reached the final stage of life—came up to me, and exchanged greetings with me. | T: Then a number of brahmins, old, aged, burdened with years, advanced in life, come to the last stage, approached me and exchanged greetings with me.
an4.22:1.3 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, they sat down to one side, and said to me: | T: When they had concluded their greetings and cordial talk, they sat down to one side and said to me:
an4.22:1.4 | S: ‘Worthy Gotama, we have heard this: | T: “‘We have heard, Master Gotama:
an4.22:1.5 | S: “The ascetic Gotama does not bow to old brahmins, elderly and senior, who are advanced in years and have reached the final stage of life; nor does he rise in their presence or offer them a seat.” | T: “The ascetic Gotama does not pay homage to brahmins who are old, aged, burdened with years, advanced in life, come to the last stage; nor does he stand up for them or offer them a seat.”
an4.22:1.6 | S: And this is indeed the case, | T: This is indeed true,
an4.22:1.7 | S: for the worthy Gotama does not bow to old brahmins, elderly and senior, who are advanced in years and have reached the final stage of life; nor does he rise in their presence or offer them a seat. | T: for Master Gotama does not pay homage to brahmins who are old, aged, burdened with years, advanced in life, come to the last stage; nor does he stand up for them or offer them a seat.
an4.22:1.8 | S: This is not appropriate, worthy Gotama.’ | T: This is not proper, Master Gotama.’
an4.22:2.1 | S: Then it occurred to me, | T: “It then occurred to me:
an4.22:2.2 | S: ‘These venerables don’t know what a senior is, or what qualities make you a senior.’ | T: These venerable ones do not know what an elder is or what the qualities that make one an elder are.
an4.22:2.3 | S: Mendicants, suppose you’re eighty, ninety, or a hundred years old. | T: Even though someone is old—eighty, ninety, or a hundred years from birth—
an4.22:2.4 | S: But your speech is untimely, false, meaningless, and against the teaching or training. You say things at the wrong time which are worthless, unreasonable, rambling, and unbeneficial. | T: if he speaks at an improper time, speaks falsely, speaks what is unbeneficial, speaks contrary to the Dhamma and the discipline, if at an improper time he speaks words that are worthless, unreasonable, rambling, and unbeneficial,
an4.22:2.5 | S: Then you’ll be reckoned a ‘childish senior’. | T: then he is reckoned as a foolish childish elder.
an4.22:3.1 | S: Now suppose you’re a youth, young, with pristine black hair, blessed with youth, in the prime of life. | T: “But even though someone is young, a youth with black hair, endowed with the blessing of youth, in the prime of life,
an4.22:3.2 | S: But your speech is timely, true, meaningful, and in line with the teaching and training. You say things at the right time which are valuable, reasonable, succinct, and beneficial. | T: if he speaks at a proper time, speaks what is truthful, speaks what is beneficial, speaks on the Dhamma and the discipline, and if at a proper time he speaks words that are worth recording, reasonable, succinct, and beneficial,
an4.22:3.3 | S: Then you’ll be reckoned an ‘astute senior’. | T: then he is reckoned as a wise elder.
an4.22:4.1 | S: There are these four qualities that make a senior. | T: “There are, bhikkhus, these four qualities that make one an elder.
an4.22:4.2 | S: What four? | T: What four?
an4.22:4.3 | S: A mendicant is ethical, restrained in the monastic code, conducting themselves well and resorting for alms in suitable places. Seeing danger in the slightest fault, they keep the rules they’ve undertaken. | T: (1) “Here, a bhikkhu is virtuous; he dwells restrained by the Pātimokkha, possessed of good conduct and resort, seeing danger in minute faults. Having undertaken the training rules, he trains in them.
an4.22:4.4 | S: They’re very learned, remembering and keeping what they’ve learned. These teachings are good in the beginning, good in the middle, and good in the end, meaningful and well-phrased, describing a spiritual practice that’s entirely full and pure. They are very learned in such teachings, remembering them, rehearsing them, mentally scrutinizing them, and penetrating them theoretically. | T: (2) “He has learned much, remembers what he has learned, and accumulates what he has learned. Those teachings that are good in the beginning, good in the middle, and good in the end, with the right meaning and phrasing, which proclaim the perfectly complete and pure spiritual life—such teachings as these he has learned much of, retained in mind, recited verbally, investigated with the mind, and penetrated well by view.
an4.22:4.5 | S: They get the four absorptions—blissful meditations in this life that belong to the higher mind—when they want, without trouble or difficulty. | T: (3) “He is one who gains at will, without trouble or difficulty, the four jhānas that constitute the higher mind and are pleasant dwellings in this very life.
an4.22:4.6 | S: They realize the undefiled freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom. And they live having realized it with their own insight in this very life due to the ending of defilements. | T: (4) “With the destruction of the taints, he has realized for himself with direct knowledge, in this very life, the taintless liberation of mind, liberation by wisdom, and having entered upon it, he dwells in it.
an4.22:4.7 | S: These are the four qualities that make a senior. | T: “These are the four qualities that make one an elder.”
an4.22:5.1 | S: The creature with a restless mind | T: The dullard with a restless mind
an4.22:5.2 | S: speaks a lot of nonsense. | T: who speaks much chatter,
an4.22:5.3 | S: Their thoughts are unsettled, | T: his thoughts unsettled,
an4.22:5.4 | S: and they don’t like the true teaching. | T: delighting in a bad teaching,
an4.22:5.5 | S: They’re far from seniority, with their bad views | T: holding bad views, disrespectful, is far from an elder’s stature.
an4.22:5.6 | S: and their lack of regard for others. | T: (none)
an4.22:6.1 | S: But one accomplished in ethics, | T: But one accomplished in virtue,
an4.22:6.2 | S: learned and eloquent, that attentive one | T: learned and discerning,
an4.22:6.3 | S: is restrained when experiencing phenomena, | T: self-controlled in the factors of firmness,
an4.22:6.4 | S: discerning the meaning with wisdom. | T: who clearly sees the meaning with wisdom;
an4.22:7.1 | S: Gone beyond all things, | T: gone beyond all phenomena,
an4.22:7.2 | S: kind, eloquent, | T: not barren, discerning;
an4.22:7.3 | S: they’ve given up birth and death, | T: who has abandoned birth and death,
an4.22:7.4 | S: and have completed the spiritual journey. | T: consummate in the spiritual life, in whom there are no taints—
an4.22:8.1 | S: That’s who I call a senior, | T: he is the one I call an elder.
an4.22:8.2 | S: who has no defilements. | T: (none)
an4.22:8.3 | S: With the ending of defilements, a mendicant | T: With the destruction of the taints

