an4.22:8.4 | S: is declared a ‘senior’.” | T: a bhikkhu is called an elder.
an4.22:8.5 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn12.67
sn12.67:1.1 | S: At one time Venerable Sāriputta and Venerable Mahākoṭṭhita were staying near Varanasi in the deer park at Isipatana. | T: On one occasion the Venerable Sāriputta and the Venerable Mahakoṭṭhita were dwelling at Baraṇasī in the Deer Park at Isipatana.
sn12.67:1.2 | S: Then in the late afternoon, Venerable Mahākoṭṭhita came out of retreat, went to Venerable Sāriputta, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then, in the evening, the Venerable Mahakoṭṭhita emerged from seclusion and approached the Venerable Sāriputta. He exchanged greetings with the Venerable Sāriputta and,
sn12.67:1.3 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to Sāriputta: | T: when they had concluded their greetings and cordial talk, he sat down to one side and said to him:
sn12.67:1.4 | S: “Well, Reverend Sāriputta, are old age and death made by oneself? Or by another? Or by both oneself and another? Or do they arise anomalously, not made by oneself or another?” | T: “How is it, friend Sāriputta: Is aging-and-death created by oneself, or is it created by another, or is it created both by oneself and by another, or has it arisen fortuitously, being created neither by oneself nor by another?”
sn12.67:1.5 | S: “No, Reverend Koṭṭhita, old age and death are not made by oneself, nor by another, nor by both oneself and another, nor do they arise anomalously, not made by oneself or another. | T: “Friend Koṭṭhita, aging-and-death is not created by oneself, nor is it created by another, nor is it created both by oneself and by another, nor has it arisen fortuitously, being created neither by oneself nor by another.
sn12.67:1.6 | S: Rather, rebirth is a requirement for old age and death.” | T: But rather, with birth as condition, aging-and-death comes to be.”
sn12.67:2.1 | S: “Well, Reverend Sāriputta, is rebirth made by oneself? Or by another? Or by both oneself and another? Or does it arise anomalously, not made by oneself or another?” | T: “How is it, friend Sāriputta: Is birth created by oneself …
sn12.67:2.2 | S: “No, Reverend Koṭṭhita, rebirth is not made by oneself, nor by another, nor by both oneself and another, nor does it arise anomalously, not made by oneself or another. | T: (none)
sn12.67:2.3 | S: Rather, continued existence is a requirement for rebirth.” | T: (none)
sn12.67:3.1 | S: “Well, Reverend Sāriputta, is continued existence made by oneself? …” … | T: Is existence …
sn12.67:3.2 | S: “Is grasping made by oneself? …” … | T: clinging …
sn12.67:3.3 | S: “Is craving made by oneself? …” … | T: craving …
sn12.67:3.4 | S: “Is feeling made by oneself? …” … | T: feeling …
sn12.67:3.5 | S: “Is contact made by oneself? …” … | T: contact …
sn12.67:3.6 | S: “Are the six sense fields made by oneself? …” … | T: the six sense bases …
sn12.67:3.7 | S: “Well, Reverend Sāriputta, are name and form made by oneself? Or by another? Or by both oneself and another? Or do they arise anomalously, not made by oneself or another?” | T: name-and-form created by oneself, or is it created by another, or is it created both by oneself and by another, or has it arisen fortuitously, being created neither by oneself nor by another?”
sn12.67:3.8 | S: “No, Reverend Koṭṭhita, name and form are not made by oneself, nor by another, nor by both oneself and another, nor do they arise anomalously, not made by oneself or another. | T: “Name-and-form, friend Koṭṭhita, is not created by oneself, nor is it created by another, nor is it created both by oneself and by another, nor has it arisen fortuitously, being created neither by oneself nor by another;
sn12.67:3.9 | S: Rather, consciousness is a requirement for name and form.” | T: but rather, with consciousness as condition, name-and-form comes to be.”
sn12.67:4.1 | S: “Well, Reverend Sāriputta, is consciousness made by oneself? Or by another? Or by both oneself and another? Or does it arise anomalously, not made by oneself or another?” | T: “How is it, friend Sāriputta: Is consciousness created by oneself, or is it created by another, or is it created both by oneself and by another, or has it arisen fortuitously, being created neither by oneself nor by another?”
sn12.67:4.2 | S: “No, Reverend Koṭṭhita, consciousness is not made by oneself, nor by another, nor by both oneself and another, nor does it arise anomalously, not made by oneself or another. | T: “Consciousness, friend Koṭṭhita, is not created by oneself, nor is it created by another, nor is it created both by oneself and by another, nor has it arisen fortuitously, being created neither by oneself nor by another;
sn12.67:4.3 | S: Rather, name and form are requirements for consciousness.” | T: but rather, with name-and-form as condition, consciousness comes to be.”
sn12.67:5.1 | S: “Just now I understood you to say: | T: “Now we understand the Venerable Sāriputta’s statement thus:
sn12.67:5.2 | S: ‘No, Reverend Koṭṭhita, name and form are not made by oneself, nor by another, nor by both oneself and another, nor do they arise anomalously, not made by oneself or another. | T: ‘Name-and-form, friend Koṭṭhita, is not created by oneself …
sn12.67:5.3 | S: Rather, consciousness is a requirement for name and form.’ | T: but rather, with consciousness as condition, name-and-form comes to be.’
sn12.67:6.1 | S: But I also understood you to say: | T: Now we also understand the Venerable Sāriputta’s other statement thus:
sn12.67:6.2 | S: ‘No, Reverend Koṭṭhita, consciousness is not made by oneself, nor by another, nor by both oneself and another, nor does it arise anomalously, not made by oneself or another. | T: ‘Consciousness, friend Koṭṭhita, is not created by oneself …
sn12.67:6.3 | S: Rather, name and form are requirements for consciousness.’ | T: but rather, with name-and-form as condition, consciousness comes to be.’
sn12.67:7.1 | S: How then should we see the meaning of this statement?” | T: But how, friend Sāriputta, should the meaning of this statement be seen?”
sn12.67:7.2 | S: “Well then, reverend, I shall give you a simile. | T: “Well then, friend, I will make up a simile for you,
sn12.67:7.3 | S: For by means of a simile some sensible people understand the meaning of what is said. | T: for some intelligent people here understand the meaning of a statement by means of a simile.
sn12.67:7.4 | S: Suppose there were two sheaves of reeds leaning up against each other. | T: Just as two sheaves of reeds might stand leaning against each other,
sn12.67:7.5 | S: In the same way, name and form are requirements for consciousness. | T: so too, with name-and-form as condition, consciousness comes to be;
sn12.67:7.6 | S: Consciousness is a requirement for name and form. | T: with consciousness as condition, name-and-form comes to be.
sn12.67:7.7 | S: Name and form are requirements for the six sense fields. | T: With name-and-form as condition, the six sense bases come to be;
sn12.67:7.8 | S: The six sense fields are requirements for contact. … | T: with the six sense bases as condition, contact….
sn12.67:7.9 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering originates. | T: Such is the origin of this whole mass of suffering.
sn12.67:7.10 | S: If the first of those sheaves of reeds were to be pulled away, the other would collapse. | T: “If, friend, one were to remove one of those sheaves of reeds, the other would fall, and if one were to remove the other sheaf, the first would fall.
sn12.67:7.11 | S: And if the other were to be pulled away, the first would collapse. | T: (none)
sn12.67:7.12 | S: In the same way, when name and form cease, consciousness ceases. | T: So too, with the cessation of name-and-form comes cessation of consciousness;
sn12.67:7.13 | S: When consciousness ceases, name and form cease. | T: with the cessation of consciousness comes cessation of name-and-form.
sn12.67:7.14 | S: When name and form cease, the six sense fields cease. | T: With the cessation of name-and-form comes cessation of the six sense bases;
sn12.67:7.15 | S: When the six sense fields cease, contact ceases. … | T: with the cessation of the six sense bases, cessation of contact….
sn12.67:7.16 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases.” | T: Such is the cessation of this whole mass of suffering.”
sn12.67:8.1 | S: “It’s incredible, Reverend Sāriputta, it’s amazing! | T: “It is wonderful, friend Sāriputta! It is amazing, friend Sāriputta!
sn12.67:8.2 | P: abbhutaṁ, āvuso sāriputta. | T: (none)
sn12.67:8.3 | S: How well spoken this was by Venerable Sāriputta! | T: How well this has been stated by the Venerable Sāriputta.
sn12.67:8.4 | S: And we can express our agreement with Venerable Sāriputta’s statement on these thirty-six grounds. | T: We rejoice in the Venerable Sāriputta’s statement on these thirty-six grounds:
sn12.67:9.1 | S: If a mendicant teaches Dhamma for disillusionment, dispassion, and cessation regarding old age and death, they’re qualified to be called ‘a mendicant who speaks on Dhamma’. | T: If, friend, a bhikkhu teaches the Dhamma for the purpose of revulsion towards aging-and-death, for its fading away and cessation, he can be called a bhikkhu who is a speaker on the Dhamma.
sn12.67:9.2 | S: If they practice for disillusionment, dispassion, and cessation regarding old age and death, they’re qualified to be called ‘a mendicant who practices in line with the teaching’. | T: If a bhikkhu is practising for the purpose of revulsion towards aging-and-death, for its fading away and cessation, he can be called a bhikkhu who is practising in accordance with the Dhamma.
sn12.67:9.3 | S: If they’re freed by not grasping by disillusionment, dispassion, and cessation regarding old age and death, they’re qualified to be called ‘a mendicant who has attained extinguishment in this very life’. | T: If through revulsion towards aging-and-death, through its fading away and cessation, a bhikkhu is liberated by nonclinging, he can be called a bhikkhu who has attained Nibbāna in this very life.
sn12.67:9.4 | S: If a mendicant teaches Dhamma for disillusionment regarding rebirth … | T: “If, friend, a bhikkhu teaches the Dhamma for the purpose of revulsion towards birth …
sn12.67:9.5 | S: continued existence … | T: existence …
sn12.67:9.6 | S: grasping … | T: clinging …
sn12.67:9.7 | S: craving … | T: craving …
sn12.67:9.8 | S: feeling … | T: feeling …
sn12.67:9.9 | S: contact … | T: contact …
sn12.67:9.10 | S: the six sense fields … | T: the six sense bases …
sn12.67:9.11 | S: name and form … | T: name-and-form …
sn12.67:9.12 | S: consciousness … | T: consciousness …
sn12.67:9.13 | S: choices … | T: volitional formations …
sn12.67:9.14 | S: If a mendicant teaches Dhamma for disillusionment, dispassion, and cessation regarding ignorance, they’re qualified to be called ‘a mendicant who speaks on Dhamma’. | T: ignorance, for its fading away and cessation, he can be called a bhikkhu who is a speaker on the Dhamma.
sn12.67:9.15 | S: If they practice for disillusionment, dispassion, and cessation regarding ignorance, they’re qualified to be called ‘a mendicant who practices in line with the teaching’. | T: If a bhikkhu is practising for the purpose of revulsion towards ignorance, for its fading away and cessation, he can be called a bhikkhu who is practising in accordance with the Dhamma.
sn12.67:9.16 | S: If they’re freed by not grasping by disillusionment, dispassion, and cessation regarding ignorance, they’re qualified to be called ‘a mendicant who has attained extinguishment in this very life’.” | T: If through revulsion towards ignorance, through its fading away and cessation, a bhikkhu is liberated by nonclinging, he can be called a bhikkhu who has attained Nibbāna in this very life.”
sn12.67:9.17 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.25
an3.25:1.1 | S: “These three individuals are found in the world. | T: “Bhikkhus, there are these three kinds of persons found existing in the world.
an3.25:1.2 | S: What three? | T: What three?
an3.25:1.3 | S: An individual with a mind like an open sore, an individual with a mind like lightning, and an individual with a mind like diamond. | T: One whose mind is like an open sore, one whose mind is like lightning, and one whose mind is like a diamond.
an3.25:1.4 | S: And who has a mind like an open sore? | T: (1) “And what, bhikkhus, is the person whose mind is like an open sore?
an3.25:1.5 | S: It’s an individual who is irritable and bad-tempered. | T: Here, some person is prone to anger and easily exasperated.
an3.25:1.6 | S: Even when lightly criticized they lose their temper, becoming annoyed, hostile, and hard-hearted, and they display annoyance, hate, and bitterness. | T: Even if he is criticized slightly he loses his temper and becomes irritated, hostile, and stubborn; he displays irritation, hatred, and bitterness.
an3.25:1.7 | S: They’re like a festering sore, which, when you hit it with a stick or a stone, discharges even more. | T: Just as a festering sore, if struck by a stick or a shard, will discharge even more matter,
an3.25:1.8 | S: In the same way, an individual is irritable and bad-tempered. | T: (none)
an3.25:1.9 | S: Even when lightly criticized they lose their temper, becoming annoyed, hostile, and hard-hearted, and they display annoyance, hate, and bitterness. | T: so too some person here is prone to anger … and displays irritation, hatred, and bitterness.
an3.25:1.10 | S: This is called an individual with a mind like an open sore. | T: This person is said to have a mind like an open sore.
an3.25:2.1 | S: And who has a mind like lightning? | T: (2) “And what is the person whose mind is like lightning?
an3.25:2.2 | S: It’s an individual who truly understands: ‘This is suffering’ … ‘This is the origin of suffering’ … ‘This is the cessation of suffering’ … ‘This is the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering’. | T: Here, some person understands as it really is: ‘This is suffering,’ and ‘This is the origin of suffering,’ and ‘This is the cessation of suffering,’ and ‘This is the way leading to the cessation of suffering.’
an3.25:2.3 | S: They’re like a person with keen eyes in the dark of the night, who sees by a flash of lightning. | T: Just as, in the dense darkness of night, a man with good sight can see forms by a flash of lightning,
an3.25:2.4 | S: In the same way, an individual truly understands: ‘This is suffering’ … ‘This is the origin of suffering’ … ‘This is the cessation of suffering’ … ‘This is the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering’. | T: so too some person here understands as it really is: ‘This is suffering’ … ‘This is the way leading to the cessation of suffering.’
an3.25:2.5 | S: This is called an individual with a mind like lightning. | T: This person is said to have a mind like lightning.
an3.25:3.1 | S: And who has a mind like diamond? | T: (3) “And what is the person whose mind is like a diamond?
an3.25:3.2 | S: It’s an individual who realizes the undefiled freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom. And they live having realized it with their own insight in this very life due to the ending of defilements. | T: Here, with the destruction of the taints, some person realizes for himself with direct knowledge, in this very life, the taintless liberation of mind, liberation by wisdom, and having entered upon it, dwells in it.
an3.25:3.3 | S: It’s like a diamond, which can’t be cut by anything at all, not even a gem or a stone. | T: Just as there is nothing that a diamond cannot cut, whether gem or stone,
an3.25:3.4 | S: In the same way, an individual realizes the undefiled freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom … in this very life … | T: so too, with the destruction of the taints, some person realizes for himself with direct knowledge … the taintless liberation of mind, liberation by wisdom, and … dwells in it.
an3.25:3.5 | S: This is called an individual with a mind like diamond. | T: This person is said to have a mind like a diamond.
an3.25:3.6 | S: These are the three individuals found in the world.” | T: “These, bhikkhus, are the three kinds of persons found existing in the world.”
an3.25:3.7 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)


